[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce any “every third packet” loss on the pc1→pc2 path or vice versa. From pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2), 60/60 ICMP echo requests were returned with 0% loss (avg RTT ~0.67 ms). The reverse (pc2→pc1) also delivered 60/60 with 0% loss. This indicates the forwarding path pc1 → router1 → router2 → pc2 is healthy.

On-device checks corroborate this: all interfaces on router1 and router2 show zero RX/TX errors and zero drops; qdisc on all involved interfaces is the default fq_codel with “dropped 0” and no netem or filters attached (tc qdisc/filter show on router1/2 and hosts). iptables/nft on routers and hosts are permissive (ACCEPT) with no ICMP or other packet-matching rules. BGP adjacencies are up and routes are correct: router1 learns 200.1.1.0/24 via router2 and router2 learns 195.11.14.0/24 via router1; kernel FIBs are consistent. There is no sign of policing, congestion, or periodic filtering that would explain a 1-in-3 drop.

Root cause: Not a network device fault in this lab; the previously observed loss is not reproducible and was likely a transient or a measurement artifact.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Recommend the reporter retest using sustained pings (e.g., 100+ probes) and, if the symptom recurs, capture simultaneous tcpdump on pc1 and pc2 to localize loss and share timestamps. Also verify no local host firewall or traffic-shaping was enabled during their measurement.
=== END ===